# Release Notes

Do **not** edit `CHANGELOG.md` during feature work. Concurrent agents conflict on that file.

Write notes to `unreleased/{feature-slug}.md` in the same change that ships a
user-, admin-, or developer-visible change. One kebab-case file per feature.
If that file already exists on this branch, append to it.

Never fold `unreleased/` into `CHANGELOG.md` unless the user explicitly asks.
When they do, run `pnpm changelog:collect` and review the result.

The format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/) and
the project uses [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/).

## Golden rules

- **Short and to the point.** One line per change. No filler.
- **Functional, not technical** — except in the Technical section.
- **Write for the reader of that section.** A public entry is read by end users;
  an admin entry by hub operators; a technical entry by developers.
- **Omit empty sections.** Only include a section when it has entries.

## File format

```markdown
### Public features
- Added: landing page shows the product flow.

### Admin features
- Changed: the FAQ search now matches article tags.

### Technical
- Extracted documentation search embedding sync into a shared helper.
```

## Writing each section

### Public features & Admin features (functional)

- Describe **what changed for the person using it**, not how it was built.
- No code, file paths, function names, or table names.
- Prefix with a Keep a Changelog verb when it adds clarity: `Added`, `Changed`,
  `Fixed`, `Removed`, `Deprecated`, `Security`.

Good:

- `Changed: cars with a too-high depreciation cost are now rejected automatically.`
- `Added: admins can set a maximum accepted price per hub.`

Avoid:

- `Refactored runSimulationEngine to extract simulationCarIsCategoryBCandidate.`
  (technical — belongs in Technical, and too detailed)

### Technical

- Here detail is welcome: noteworthy refactors, new dependencies, breaking
  internal changes.
- Call out **breaking changes** explicitly.
- **Do not** add entries that only announce a routine schema/Prisma migration.
  Mention a migration only when it is noteworthy: breaking, destructive,
  requires a data backfill, or needs operator action beyond a normal deploy.

## Versioning

- `MAJOR` — breaking change for users or integrators.
- `MINOR` — new backward-compatible feature.
- `PATCH` — backward-compatible bug fix.

Derive the entry from the change itself, not from the git log.

## Discord announcements

The notes stay English. When asked to announce a release to admins, follow
`.cursor/rules/release-announcements.mdc` (Dutch Discord post, not changelog).
